{"title": "CSCI-605: Advanced Object-Oriented Programming Concepts", "learningObjectives": [
     "Define and distinguish between inheritance and composition as mechanisms for code reuse and object design.",
     "Explain how dynamic method dispatch and encapsulation enforce runtime polymorphism and data integrity.",
     "Apply design patterns and event-driven principles to structure complex, maintainable software systems."
   ], "beats": [
     {
       "title": "Foundations of Object Identity and State",
       "claim": "Objects possess a unique runtime identity and encapsulated field state that persists across method invocations.",
       "example": "A `User` instance maintains its own `username` and `sessionToken` values independent of other `User` instances.",
       "visualDirection": "Display a side-by-side comparison of two `User` objects with distinct memory addresses and separate field values, highlighting encapsulation boundaries.",
       "sourceIds": ["java-language-specification", "python-data-model"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Inheritance versus Composition for Reuse",
       "claim": "Inheritance reuses and specializes behavior through class hierarchies, while composition connects objects through explicit attributes to promote flexible design.",
       "example": "A `Vehicle` base class provides `move()` logic, but a `DeliveryTruck` composes a `CargoContainer` object instead of inheriting from `Truck` to avoid rigid hierarchies.",
       "visualDirection": "Show a class diagram contrasting a deep inheritance tree with a flat composition graph, using arrows to indicate 'has-a' relationships.",
       "sourceIds": ["java-language-specification", "python-data-model"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Dynamic Method Dispatch and Polymorphism",
       "claim": "Dynamic method dispatch selects an implementation from the runtime class of the receiver, enabling polymorphic behavior without compile-time type knowledge.",
       "example": "Calling `draw()` on a `Shape` reference invokes `Circle.draw()` or `Square.draw()` depending on the actual object at runtime.",
       "visualDirection": "Illustrate a method call flow where a generic `Shape` reference routes to distinct `Circle` and `Square` implementations, emphasizing runtime resolution.",
       "sourceIds": ["java-language-specification"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Encapsulation and Access Control",
       "claim": "Access modifiers control which declarations are visible, ensuring that internal state remains protected and only authorized operations modify it.",
       "example": "A `BankAccount` class exposes `deposit()` and `withdraw()` methods while keeping the `balance` field private to prevent unauthorized modification.",
       "visualDirection": "Render a class interface with public methods highlighted and private fields shaded, showing how access control restricts direct state access.",
       "sourceIds": ["java-language-specification", "python-data-model"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Exceptions for Error Handling",
       "claim": "Exceptions separate normal results from abrupt completion, allowing structured error propagation without interrupting normal control flow.",
       "example": "A `NetworkRequest` throws a `ConnectionTimeoutException` when a server fails to respond, allowing the caller to handle the failure gracefully.",
       "visualDirection": "Map a control flow diagram showing a normal execution path branching into an exception handler block when a timeout occurs.",
       "sourceIds": ["java-language-specification", "python-data-model"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Generics for Type-Safe Abstractions",
       "claim": "Generic types express reusable abstractions while preserving compile-time type constraints, preventing runtime type mismatches in collections and utilities.",
       "example": "A `Queue<T>` class ensures that only elements of type `T` are added or removed, eliminating the need for runtime casting.",
       "visualDirection": "Show a generic collection diagram with type parameters `T` and `U`, highlighting how compile-time checks enforce type safety.",
       "sourceIds": ["java-language-specification"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Event-Driven Programming Principles",
       "claim": "Event-driven programming decouples components by allowing objects to react to asynchronous occurrences through registered callbacks and message queues.",
       "example": "A `UIController` registers a `Button` instance to emit `click` events, triggering a `EventHandler` without direct method calls.",
       "visualDirection": "Draw a publisher-subscriber diagram showing an event source broadcasting to multiple independent listeners via a message bus.",
       "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-605"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Design Patterns for Structural Integrity",
       "claim": "Design patterns provide proven structural templates that solve recurring design problems by standardizing object interactions and system architecture.",
       "example": "The `Strategy` pattern allows a `PaymentProcessor` to swap `CreditCard` and `Crypto` algorithms at runtime without modifying the core class.",
       "visualDirection": "Present a before-and-after class diagram showing how a rigid hierarchy is replaced by a flexible strategy interface and implementations.",
       "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-605"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Concurrent and Distributed Programming Foundations",
       "claim": "Concurrent programming manages simultaneous execution through shared memory or message passing, while distributed programming coordinates independent processes across networked or-",
       "example": "A `TaskScheduler` uses thread pools to process independent jobs concurrently, ensuring that shared resources are accessed via synchronized locks or immutable data structures.",
       "visualDirection": "Render a concurrent execution model with multiple threads accessing a shared resource, highlighting synchronization mechanisms like mutexes or atomic variables.",
       "sourceIds": ["java-language-specification", "rit-csci-605"]
     },
     {
       "title": "Practical Analysis Checklist for Implementation",
       "claim": "Effective implementation requires verifying that responsibilities are explicit, invariants are maintained, dependencies are minimized, and substitution behavior is preserved.",
       "example": "Before deploying a `DataProcessor`, developers check that each class has a single responsibility, validates that internal state cannot be corrupted, and confirms that subclasses can replace base classes without breaking contracts.",
       "visualDirection": "Display a checklist graphic with checkboxes for 'Single Responsibility', 'Invariant Validation', 'Dependency Minimization', and 'Substitution Compliance', ready for instructor review.",
       "sourceIds": ["java-language-specification", "python-data-model"]
     }
   ]}
